    "result_text": "The build passes. The acceptance test suite exercised the new path \u2014 the debug log shows `Duplicate owner email at POST /api/owners: An owner with email 'owner5@example.test' already exists` producing a 409.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented owner-creation rejection when the lower-cased email is already used by another owner, returning **409 Conflict**, by mirroring the existing telephone-uniqueness design so no single class became a catch-all:\n\n- **`OwnerRepository.findByEmail`** \u2014 new query method, with implementations in all three repository backends:\n  - `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl` (JPQL), `SpringDataOwnerRepository` (`@Query`), and `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` (named-param SQL).\n- **`ClinicService.findOwnerByEmail`** + **`ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 read-only service pass-through, matching `findOwnerByTelephone`.\n- **`OwnerEmailUniquenessValidator`** (new `@Component`) \u2014 single-responsibility check; skips a `null` email since email is optional, and throws on a match.\n- **`DuplicateOwnerEmailException`** (new) \u2014 the domain signal for the conflict.\n- **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 new handler mapping the exception to 409 with an `errors: [\"email\"]` body, consistent with the telephone/household handlers.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 injects the validator and calls `validateUnique(owner.getEmail())` right after telephone validation. Email is already normalized (lower-cased) upstream via `EmailNormalizer.normalize`, so comparison happens on the canonical stored form \u2014 two emails differing only in case are treated as duplicates.\n\nSince email is optional, absent emails are never treated as duplicates.",
